			"contents": "Volume 1, Chronicle 1\nThe Halo\n\nThe sun surface from under the thick clouds.\nThe ray casts shadows to the fluffy sea in the sky.\nThis is literally heaven on earth.\nIt is 4:56am, and Art is already up and dressed for work.\nHe does his morning ritual: \nquiet meditation for 15 minutes,\nchanting spiritual codes for another 15,\nconcluding with a deep bow to the sun \nwith palm pressed together in front of his chest - a posture known as Gasho\nconnecting his body and spirit.\nArt, a young boy with sharp eyes and a shaved head,\nis a monk living on the top of Mount Fuji\nat the Sengen Shrine's satellite site.\nEach day he performs his duty of cleaning,\nchecking, and charging the robots.\nOccassionally, their repairs as well.\nThe robots would in turn do the cleaning,\ngardening, and cooking for the two monks living there.\nEvery now and then, building maintenance as well.\n\nToday is a special day. \nToday is Art's 12th birthday.\nAlthough monks hardly celebrate their birthdays,\nfor the two of them living together in the remote mountain top\nwith hardly any visitors anymore,\nthey have learned that to entertain themselves\nand keeping their mental and physical wellness in shape,\nis as much their duty as the daily rituals and chores.\n\nAs Art finishes up with the third and last robot,\nhe sets it into operation.\nHe runs back to his room, where his roommate Leon works,\njust finishing with his prompts to instruct the robots of\nevery detail to prepare for this special day.\nLeon is much more than a roommate to Art.\nBeing 20 years older, Leon is like a big brother slash father to Art.\nFor Art has never met his birth parents. \nHe was rescued by Leon from\nan emergency pod 12 years ago, in a desert, \nonly hours after he was born,\nminutes before he would have been dead.\nThat changed the course of Leon's life, to say the least.\nLeon, once a respected Beast Hunter,\nbecame a monk, to stay away from the front line,\nto try to live long, so he could see Art grow up.\n12 years have gone by, and as this young boy runs towards him from behind\nand jumps onto his thick back, even his stern face\ncracked up into a genuine smile.\n\nIt is 7am, and they have both finished their work for the day.\nToday, Leon has promised to teach Art level 2 combat skills.\nEven though Leon has at first wanted to keep Art away from the frontiers,\nhe has to admit that Art is actually quite talented,\nphysically fit, and determined; \nSince two years ago, Art has just about cleared every hurdle\nthat Leon has set to deter his ask.\nThey change into their crisp dry training gi,\nthat feels ice cold everytime one puts them on,\nand walk out to the flat ground in front of the Shrine.\n\n\"Art, recite to me the 5 core skills of Level 1.\" Leon commands.\n\"Punchs. Kicks. Throws. Locks. Fakes.\"\n\"The 3 contexts\" asked Leon\n\"Dense, Sparse and Mobile\" Art replies.\n\"The one contradiction\" Leon pressed.\n\"The ultimate logic is intuition, and the critical bias is faith\" Art replies.\nLeon nods.\n\"So you have them memorized, but not quite applied.\" Leon mocks Art, who humbly responds with a Gasho and a blush.\n\"And that's fine. That's how we learn.\nSomethings we learn and apply immediately, \nsomethings we learn, memorize, and only until one day in the future, \nwe apply them without knowing\"\n\"So remember this, today we unlock Level 2, but that does not mean you can stop practicing Level 1. \nIt only means you are now ready for more complexity.\"\n\"Hai!\" Art agrees excitedly.\n\nAnd so they trained, in the thin air at 3700m elevation,\namoungst the volcanic rocks,\nin front of the stone walls of the shrine,\nBetween the wooden Torii that mark the gateways for gods.\nBetween the strikes and blocks,\nthe deep breathes and the strong heart beats\nthat pumps far more oxygen\nthan a laymen at sea level,\nthey were laughing and panting,\nsweats flowing down their backs\nas time flows past.\nAnd when they finally had enough, \nthe sun was already setting, on the other side of the moountain.\n\nAs they come back to their spotless room,\nthey took turns to have a mist shower which recycles the water after each use.\nAs they came up fresh, with their bodies aching,\ndinner was ready:\n3D printed Tofu with a small portion of lab-grown cabbage pickles,\nand air-fried pumpkin tempura, Art's favorite.\nIt is customary to have their meal in silence,\nto give full respect for the food on the table.\nWith so much artificial production, though,\nArt has always wondered whether the respect for 'life' \nis appropriate.\nNonetheless, Art sees it as a silence for gratefulness.\nHe would have died 12 years ago, baked well-done in the heat\nof the desert, were it not for Leon.\n\nThey finished their meal in sync,\nand as they are paying their respect to the food,\nthe light goes out.\nNo, a cake did not to come out. Nor a robot choir.\nsomething starts glowing, illuminating the darkness.\nHeld in the palm of Leon, light casts highlights on the distinct features of the two faces,\nLeon explains,\n\"Art, there is something I have kept for you.\"\nArt looked closely at the glowing object, that looked just like a donut, which he has never had.\n\"This Halo is a memory capsule. It came with you when I found you 12 years ago.\"\nLeon continues,\n\"It is from someone important to you. \nAnd he has instructed whomever found you to only share this with you\nonly when you have a strong body, and a critical mind.\nI figured there's a timelimit to the relevance of this message, too,\nso I have decided that I will give it to you today.\"\n\nWith that, Leon hands over the Halo to Art.\n\"This is your father's gift for you.\"\n\n\n",
